I agree. I attach the script I used for testing this for various
size's.

there are two issues. one is this is an artifact of INLA itself, for
which the only way to check this properly is run a long mcmc for each
case. the second, if this is generically related to 'size', since
size=1 is know to be a hard case. for large 'size' then bin->gauss
hence there is no approximation error any more.

the script attached, run this for size=1:10, and the coverage doesn't
seem to bad to me.

